CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is explicit, but a visible filtering difference occurs only when runtime tree data contains overloaded objects, a non-null filter is active, and the object's two names differ in how configured patterns match them. Those runtime values are not shown.

Object filtering now supports multiple candidate names. Navigator tree loading supplies both the normal object name and overloaded name for overloaded database objects, allowing either name to satisfy an inclusion rule while a match on either name triggers an exclusion rule. The public filter method's Java signature also changes from a single String parameter to String varargs.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When a navigator child is an overloaded database object and a filter is active, its normal name and overloaded name are both considered. An inclusion pattern may match either name, while an exclusion pattern matching either name rejects the child. Non-overloaded children continue to be filtered using only their normal name.
  • Callers of DBSObjectFilter.matches can now submit multiple candidate names in one call; each pattern is treated as matching when it matches at least one candidate name.

Investigate
  • Find all source and binary consumers of DBSObjectFilter.matches, especially consumers outside this plugin, and determine whether they are rebuilt together with this change.
  • Trace how navigator filters are created and when loadTreeItems encounters DBPOverloadedObject instances, including the naming contracts implemented by those objects.
  • Inspect implementations and contracts of DBPOverloadedObject.getOverloadedName and the complete matching implementation for null handling and naming formats.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not show every source caller of DBSObjectFilter.matches, so it is unknown whether other callers will supply multiple names or require adaptation.
  • It is not established whether DBSObjectFilter.matches is a supported cross-plugin or third-party callable boundary whose binary compatibility must be maintained.
  • The actual observable navigator change depends on an active filter and on loaded child data containing DBPOverloadedObject instances with names that produce different matching results.
  • The nullability and formatting contract of DBPOverloadedObject.getOverloadedName is not supplied, so the practical null and normalization behavior cannot be established.
  • The intended business rule for conflicts where inclusion matches one candidate name and exclusion matches another is not documented in the supplied context.

